# FactLattice — System Overview [SYSTEM:FL-OVERVIEW]

> This corpus is FactLattice specified in FactLattice. Every claim below about
> queryability is testable against this document set: parse it, lint it, traverse it,
> render it. If the format could not carry its own specification, the claim that it
> carries yours would be unsupported.

---

## [FL-THESIS] Graph primacy — the thesis

- part-of: [FL-OVERVIEW]
- status: draft
- importance: foundational

The knowledge graph is the primary artifact of the domain it describes. Source code,
diagrams, test plans, runbooks, and business documents are derived instantiations of it,
not the source of truth. The model is domain-general; software engineering is its
best-measured application, not its boundary.

The practical consequence is the whole point: when the graph is primary, divergence
between what is specified and what exists becomes a **detectable, triageable condition**
rather than inevitable decay. A diagram that was pasted into a document drifts silently.
A diagram rendered from the graph on request cannot drift, because it is not stored. A
requirement whose implementing module was deleted becomes a dangling edge that a linter
reports, not a paragraph nobody re-read.

FactLattice does not claim that a graph is a better way to write documents. It claims
that once relationships are declared explicitly and typed, questions that were previously
answered by reading become questions that are answered by traversal — and traversal is
mechanical, complete, and repeatable in a way that reading is not.

---

## [FL-SCOPE] Scope — what FactLattice is and is not

- part-of: [FL-OVERVIEW]
- see-also: [FL-THESIS]
- status: draft

FactLattice **is** three things that ship together:

- **A notation.** `.lts` documents that encode typed nodes, typed edges, properties, and
  four graph layers. Specified in [FL-MODEL].
- **An engine.** `lts-engine`: four composed API layers over one graph index and one
  freshness gate. Specified in [FL-ENGINE].
- **A methodology.** Machine authorship, declared relationships, recorded decisions with
  their invalidating assumptions, and pluggable domain vocabulary. Specified in
  [FL-PROFILES] and [FL-DECISIONS].

FactLattice **is not** a database, a wiki, an embedding index, or a documentation
generator. It has no query planner, no storage engine of its own, and no inference step.
It reads a directory of text files, builds an in-memory index, and answers questions
about it deterministically.

---

## [FL-STACK] The stack — notation, engine, methodology

- part-of: [FL-OVERVIEW]
- depends-on: [FL-MODEL]
- depends-on: [FL-ENGINE]
- status: draft

The notation is inert without the engine, and the engine is arbitrary without the
methodology. All three are in scope of this corpus:

| Part | Artifact | Specified in |
|------|----------|--------------|
| Notation | `.lts` documents; nodes, edges, properties, layers | [FL-MODEL] |
| Engine | QUERY, ANALYTICS, LINT, RENDER; the freshness kernel | [FL-ENGINE], [FL-FRESHNESS] |
| Methodology | Domain profiles, namespace tiers, decision records | [FL-PROFILES], [FL-DECISIONS] |

A reader evaluating FactLattice for adoption should read [FL-THESIS], then [FL-ENGINE]
(to see what questions the corpus can answer), then [FL-FRESHNESS] (to see what happens
when the corpus and the answer disagree). [FL-MODEL] is reference material; it is read
when authoring, not when evaluating.

---

## [FL-AUTHORSHIP] Machine-authored, human-reviewed

- part-of: [FL-OVERVIEW]
- see-also: [FL-THESIS]
- see-also: [DEC-MACHINE-AUTHORED]
- status: draft

Humans do not hand-edit `.lts` documents. An Intelligence composes them during an
interactive session; humans read rendered output — a documentation site, a dashboard, a
rendered diagram. The `.lts` extension is deliberately unmapped on developer machines.

This is a design constraint, not a preference, and it buys two things. First, density: a
document optimized for machine extraction can be far denser than one optimized for a
human's first read, because the human never reads the source. Second, discipline: every
relationship must be **declared** at authoring time. That declaration is what makes
query-time traversal deterministic — see [INV-DETERMINISTIC].

